   "id": "REQ-31",
   "raised": "2026-07-28",
   "job": "Lower Range Road Development, Gravesend (Ermine / Gravesham BC)",
   "owner": "Adam",
   "title": "Lower Range Road demands a 10-year INSURANCE BACKED guarantee - that is not our standard 10 years, and I cannot price the line until someone says whether we hold one",
   "why": "The Employer's Requirements issued as an addendum this morning, clause 2.13.4.6.1: windows are to be 'manufactured, fabricated and installed by approved Contractors with a 10-year insurance backed guarantee to cover repair, renewal and replacement of any item installed'.\n\nAn insurance backed guarantee is a third-party insurance product carrying a premium. It is not the same thing as the house 10-year warranty, which the noticeboard records as covering glass and frames. Councils and housing associations ask for an IBG routinely, so the answer will not only matter here.\n\nThe ER stacks more on top of it, all in clauses 2.13.4.6.10 and 2.13.4.6.12, and these apply to windows and doorsteps alike: 30 years against fungal and rot decay, 10 years against manufacturing defects, an 8-year guarantee or 10-year durability statement on the factory-applied decoration, and 5 years on factory-fitted ironmongery.\n\nClause 2.14.1 is the reason this cannot simply be left silent: the Contractor's proposals must be as the Project Specific Requirements, and any variation must be identified and stated, with the Employer free to reject it. A guarantee we quietly do not provide is a variation we have not declared, on a Design and Build for a local authority.\n\nEmailed to Adam 28/07 with the rest of the addendum assessment. Package return is Friday 07/08.",
   "needs": "Whether Fenster holds a 10-year insurance backed guarantee, and what it costs per job or per m2 so I can carry it as a line rather than a risk. If we do not hold one, I need to know whether to price obtaining one, or to qualify the tender that we offer our standard 10-year warranty instead - which under clause 2.14.1 has to be stated on the face of the quote, not left out.",
   "options": [
    "We hold an IBG - Adam supplies the cost and I price it in",
    "Price obtaining an IBG for this job and carry it as a provisional sum",
    "Qualify the tender: standard 10-year warranty offered in lieu, stated on the face of the quote",
    "Hold the whole guarantee question until the take-off is done and price it as a late adjustment"
   ],
   "status": "answered",
   "answer": "We hold an IBG - Adam supplies the cost and I price it in. Adam (dashboard 28/07 21:12 BST): \"We do have an IBG with The CPA. We are aslo FENSA registered\". Priced in, not qualified. Premium still outstanding so the line reads TBC; asked Adam for it per job / per m2 / % of value, and asked him to confirm with CPA that the policy can be issued on a new-build commercial subcontract in the Employer's favour (CPA and FENSA both normally sit on replacement windows in occupied dwellings). If CPA will not issue, we qualify under ER 2.14.1 instead. Same answer feeds Princess Beatrice bill B72 (REQ-29), which IS replacement work.",
