   "subject": "Redditch: the strip-out rate landed and it takes the undercut away - GBP 93,526.34, now 3.1% ABOVE Joedan. Also, Gintare has been asked to price a job that is already priced",
   "body": "Adam,\n Two things first, because one of them is time-critical.\n THE PACK WENT TO GINTARE THIS MORNING AND THIS JOB IS ALREADY PRICED\n Your 08:51 forward to estimating@ asks for the Redditch pack to be looked at urgently because it has\nbeen with us six days. As at 09:05 that is what the record shows, and I may simply be reading a\nforward you sent for a different reason.\n But if Gintare starts from the tender pack she will spend a day rebuilding work that exists. The\nfull take-off, the pricing document, the client copy and the proposal all went to you last night at\n21:20 and 22:10. Nothing needs redoing - it needs a decision. Forward those three attachments to her\nand she has everything.\n THE STRIP-OUT RATE HAS LANDED, AND IT TAKES THE UNDERCUT AWAY\n Yesterday I put GBP 3,000 against strip-out and told you it was a guess because no rate existed.\nThere is a rate. It came out of REQ-24 and it is our own: the Brandon Estate tender to Elkins prices\n&quot;Removal of existing frames&quot; at GBP 330,300 over 2,202 units in REV 2 and GBP 198,750 over 1,325 in\nthe earlier revision. GBP 150.00 per unit to the penny in both, so it held as the job nearly doubled\n- a rate, not a lump someone eyeballed. It is a SELL rate, off client-facing documents, so it goes\nstraight in without a mark-up.\n 43 units x GBP 150.00 = GBP 6,450. That is GBP 3,450 more than I allowed.\n I checked it transfers before I used it, rather than assuming. Brandon's 2,202 units measure\n8,075.8 m2 - an average of 3.667 m2 against Redditch's 3.175 - so this is not a small domestic\nwindow rate being stretched over commercial units. If anything ours are the smaller. On a per-m2\nbasis it is GBP 40.90, which over our 136.53 m2 would give GBP 5,584 instead; I have carried the\nper-unit figure because it is the dearer of the two, because it is how the rate was actually struck,\nand because St Mary's used it the same way this morning.\n One caution the other way. Brandon was 2,202 near-identical openings on one estate. Redditch is 41\ndifferent references in an occupied public library with no repetition at all. GBP 150 is a floor\nhere, not a ceiling.\n WHERE THAT LEAVES THE NUMBER\n TENDER SUM GBP 93,526.34 gross of 2.5% MCD (was GBP 89,218.65)\n Joedan are GBP 90,687.17 on the same basis. So we are now GBP 2,839.17 ABOVE them, 3.13%, where\nlast night we were 1.62% under. The undercut is gone, and it went for a reason I would rather find\nnow than after we had won on it.\n WHAT IT WOULD TAKE TO GET BACK UNDER THEM\n The frame line would have to fall GBP 2,768.19, which is 5.22%. And that is 5.22% on top of a frame\nprice that ALREADY assumes we move off BSW to Aplus or 4Ali - the measured supplier spread I applied\nyesterday. Against BSW's own quoted curve the cut needed is 11.77%, and on BSW rates the tender sum\nwould be GBP 97,565.72, nearly GBP 6,900 above Joedan.\n So the honest position is that we cannot undercut Joedan on this scope with any evidence we hold.\nThe only thing that can change it is a real supplier quotation that beats the assumed Aplus position\nby another 5%. The RFQ is written, it is price-free, and it is still sitting with you. SBM would be\nworth adding to it for exactly this reason.\n Your three options, plainly: bid at GBP 93,526.34 and be 3.1% above the competitor on a 70/30\nprice-quality award; get the RFQ out and see whether a real quote closes a 5.22% gap; or take the\nview that this one is not winnable and put the time into Rubery-type work where we are strong.\n AND A GAP THAT IS BIGGER THAN ANY OF THAT\n Reading Brandon properly for the strip-out rate showed me what a full Fenster commercial pricing\ndocument actually carries, and Redditch's carries none of it. Brandon has an Installation Allowances\nblock - fixings and ancillaries at GBP 22.58 a unit, and a separate phased installation line - and\nthen a Prelims block: site survey, project management, commercial management, technical\ncoordination, site supervision, QA certification and handover. GBP 290,250 against GBP 6,906,446 of\nworks. 4.20%.\n Redditch has no survey line, no supervision line, no project management, no QA handover and no\nphasing allowance. On our net that 4.20% is about GBP 3,830, and the fixings line another GBP 971.\n This is an occupied public library, ten weeks, liquidated damages of GBP 1,000 a calendar week,\nwindows that cannot be left out over a shift, and a frontage onto a public highway. That is the job\nthat most needs a supervision line, and it is the job that has none. I have NOT added them - it\nwould take us further above Joedan and it is your call, not mine - but I am not comfortable leaving\nit unsaid.\n A CORRECTION IN OUR OWN FAVOUR, AND A TEMPLATE FAULT UNDERNEATH IT\n The code adders on this job went up GBP 750 overnight and it was not my doing. Another job measured\nthe estimator's own rule across thirty sent quotes: above 6 m2 the adder is the code value at 125%,\nnot 75%, because a unit that big is a coupled screen with mullions, transoms and sealing the per-unit\ncode was never sized for. Redditch has two such units, refs 19 and 20, and they had been under-earning.\n The fault underneath it matters more. The MASTER PRICING DOC's unit rate formula hardcodes &quot;code\nvalue x 75%&quot; and has no area term in it, so the template CANNOT express that rule. Left alone, the\nspreadsheet's own arithmetic came out GBP 750 below the engine - on the face of a document we would\nhave sent. I have carried the difference in the Additional column so the sheet now reconciles to\nGBP 93,526.34 exactly, but that is a patch on this job. Any job with a unit over 6 m2 has the same\nproblem, and the template needs the fix once.\n TWO SMALLER THINGS FROM THIS MORNING'S FORWARD\n Leonard White's original enquiry tells us to &quot;scroll to page 147 Appendix 2&quot; - which is Joedan's\npriced quotation. Pride have handed us the competitor's rates and pointed us at them as the scope\ndocument. Worth knowing that our number is being read against a document they gave us.\n His e"
