   "body": "Adam,\n Your four points, in order. The pricing document and proposal went over separately a few minutes ago.\n 1. STRIP OUT INCLUDED - AGREED, AND I HAVE FOUND OUR OWN PRECEDENT FOR IT\n\n It is in the document I have just sent you, at a GBP 3,000 allowance, GBP 69.77 an opening.\n And chasing Rubery Library turned up something better than my allowance. Our own issued quotation\nto Pride for Rubery Library, 21/10/2025, GBP 24,096.72 ex VAT, says this on the front page:\n &quot;All prices include installation and removal of old frames.&quot;\n That is the first Fenster document I have found anywhere that puts removal of old frames inside the\nprice in writing - and it is the same client, the same building type, and a job we won. So the\nposition you have taken tonight is the position we already took with Pride. I have stopped calling\nstrip-out an open question.\n What I still do not have is a RATE. Rubery does not break it out - it is inside the frame prices -\nso I cannot difference it without inventing a number. REQ-24 stays open for the rate itself, not\nfor the principle.\n 2. RUBERY LIBRARY AND MERTHYR TYDFIL - BOTH CONFIRMED, AND OUR RECORDS HAD NEITHER\n\n Both are in the archive under 2. Projects\\2. Completed\\Pride Developments. Rubery has a full O&M\nmanual issued. 92-94 High Street is there as &quot;92-94 High Street (BARCODE)&quot;. Catherine's House,\nPlymouth is a third completed Pride job, and RAF Mildenhall is live.\n Worth knowing why I did not have this last night: Pride have 19 rows on the Estimating Log and\nEVERY win/loss cell is blank. Four completed jobs and the log records none of them. That is why I\ntold you I could not give you a win rate on Pride - it was not caution, the data is not there.\n Across the whole log it is 327 jobs and 24 with any win/loss mark, so 7.3%. I have written the four\nPride wins into the Redditch job record and onto the noticeboard so the other chats have them. I\nhave NOT been able to put them on the hub Scoreboard: outcomes live in a table I can only write\nthrough a deploy, and the hub deploy has been locked since 19:56 by an npm cache conflict with\nanother session. It will go up with the next clean deploy.\n RUBERY IS THE COMPARATOR I SHOULD HAVE HAD. Same client, same building type, a library, and won.\nBetter than the Severn Trent quote I used, which is a different building for the same client. I\nhave not re-priced Redditch on it tonight because Rubery is a mixed job - Liniar uPVC casements for\nthe small windows, aluminium only on the four big screens - so it needs unpicking properly rather\nthan quickly. It is the first thing I will do when the RFQ position is settled.\n 3. HOW I WOULD PRICE JOBS DIFFERENTLY - RECOMMENDATION ONLY, I HAVE CHANGED NO CODING\n\n Two things are wrong with the current system and I can put numbers on both.\n THE RATE REGISTER IS A BAND MEDIAN AND IT IS WRONG AT BOTH ENDS. Measured on two independent jobs\nnow, St Mary's and Severn Trent, different suppliers and dates:\n band St Mary's Severn Trent\nunder 1.5 m2 -35.5% -38.9%\n1.5-3 m2 -1.2% -20.4%\n3-6 m2 +37.5% +18.1%\nover 6 m2 +35.2% +34.1%\n Positive means we are ABOVE the real price. We under-price small units by about a third and\nover-price large ones by about a third. On a broad mix they cancel, which is exactly why whole-job\nnumbers have always looked respectable while per-window numbers were not.\n WHAT I WOULD DO INSTEAD. Stop taking a median for a band and fit a curve to real recent supplier\nquotes instead - rate per m2 against area. I did it on the BSW Severn Trent quote and six points\ngave an R-squared of 0.9934, which is as close to a straight line as this trade gets. One curve per\nsupplier per system, refitted whenever a new quote lands. It costs nothing, it uses quotes we\nalready hold, and it removes the band cliff entirely.\n THE CODE ADDER IS A FIXED SUM PER UNIT, SO OUR MARGIN COLLAPSES AS WINDOWS GET BIGGER. On Redditch\nthe adder is 50.7% of the frame line under 1.5 m2, 38.6% at 1.5-3, 19.0% at 3-6 and 12.2% over 6.\nRedditch averages 3.18 m2 a unit and earns 24.7%. Crestwood Park averaged 1.29 m2 and earned 42.9%.\nSame template, same rules, nearly double the margin, purely because the windows were smaller.\n WHAT I WOULD DO INSTEAD. Make the adder a percentage of the frame value with a floor - so a small\nwindow still carries its minimum handling cost, and a big one still earns proportionally. Set the\npercentage so the small end lands where it does today and nothing changes on the jobs we already\nprice well. That is a one-line change to the template and it is yours to approve, not mine to make.\n The third thing is not a formula. Fenster has two pricing routes that do not agree: the MASTER\nPRICING DOC with its per-unit labour codes, and whatever produced the Rubery quotation, which\ncarries labour as a single GBP 1,620 ancillary line. Same client, same year. Whichever we keep, it\nshould be one of them.\n 4. SBM - THEY ARE REAL, THEY ARE CHEAP-LOOKING, AND I CANNOT GIVE YOU A RATE YET\n\n SBM Glazing Ltd, 418-420 London Road, High Wycombe, company 12083999. A Cortizo fabricator. Raj\nquotes estimating@ direct. They are NOT on our Suppliers Listing - we have been using them job by\njob. Three documents:\n Raglan School, 26/06/2026, quoted to estimating@ - 5 aluminium windows, UNGLAZED, white,\nGBP 2,500, lead time 2-3 weeks.\nWelland Place, 11/05/2026 - 1 window UNGLAZED RAL 7005, GBP 500.\nPincents Kiln, 07/05/2026 - GBP 8,560, 4 windows plus 1 commercial door, GLAZED, supply only.\n Here is why I am not giving you a comparison off those. Two of the three are UNGLAZED, which is a\ndifferent product from the glazed rates Redditch is priced on, and the third has no schedule I can\ntie to it - the drawings and glass order in that folder are dated a MONTH after the proforma and\ncover twelve positions, not five, so pairing them would manufacture a rate rather than find one.\nThat is the same trap as the strip-out &quot;rates&quot; that turned out to be item reference numbers.\n WHAT I WOULD DO: pu"
