   "body": "Three questions, three answers, and one of them is uncomfortable. Updated workbook attached with a\nnew Margin & Confidence sheet, plus a separate clean RFQ schedule you can send to a supplier today.\n SHORT VERSION\n Am I confident? In the TAKE-OFF, yes - quantities, sizes, configurations, all 43 items. In the\nPRICE, no, not to the standard needed to commit to beating a competitor. I can now put a number on\nhow unsure: roughly plus or minus 6%, and I found real evidence to narrow it.\n Profit: GBP 19,875 gross on materials, 22.1% of the sell, IF fitting breaks even.\n Undercut Joedan: at my benchmark we cannot. We are already GBP 1,491 OVER their net before a penny\nof strip-out. On better evidence we get about GBP 2,065 of headroom, which is GBP 48 per opening to\nstrip 43 windows out of an occupied library. That is tight, not comfortable.\n 1. THE PROFIT\n Frame supply, COST 60,551.26\nSolar-control glass premium, COST 1,814.51\n= material we buy 62,365.77\nHouse code adders, MARGIN 19,875.00\nInstallation, revenue 7,670.00\nSELL 89,910.77\n Gross margin GBP 19,875.00 - 22.1% of the sell, 31.9% mark-up on the material.\n That is NOT net profit, and I do not want to give you a number that pretends to be. It carries no\nprelims, no supervision, no survey time, no main contractor's discount, and no strip-out. And the\ninstallation line is revenue, not margin: what fitting actually costs Fenster is not recorded\nanywhere I can see, so I have assumed it breaks even. If fitting runs at a loss on this job the\n22.1% is the ceiling, not the answer.\n 2. WHY THIS JOB EARNS LESS THAN YOU MIGHT EXPECT - AND IT IS THE TEMPLATE, NOT THE PRICING\n\n I think this is the most useful thing I found today, and it applies to every job we price.\n The house adder is a FIXED SUM PER UNIT - SAW 337.50, MAW 412.50, and so on. So the bigger the\nwindow, the smaller that fixed sum is as a proportion. On this job:\n under 1.5 m2 11 items adder is 50.7% of the frame line\n1.5 - 3 m2 10 items adder is 38.6%\n3 - 6 m2 20 items adder is 19.0%\nover 6 m2 2 items adder is 12.2%\n Redditch averages 3.18 m2 a unit, so the adder is 24.7% of the frame line overall.\n Crestwood Park averaged 1.29 m2 a unit and the adder there was 42.9% - GBP 20,550 on a GBP 27,329.60\nBSW buy, both figures checked against the quote that went out.\n Same template, same rules, nearly double the margin - purely because the units were smaller. This\njob is big-unit work, so the template hands us a thin margin automatically. That is worth knowing\nbefore we discount anything: there is less fat here to give away than on a normal schools job.\n 3. HOW CONFIDENT I AM, AND WHAT I DID ABOUT IT\n You told me to search the old jobs, so I did, and I found the single best comparator we own.\n BSW quotation QT250834, dated 15 June 2026 - Sheerline Prestige aluminium casements, factory glazed,\ntrickle vents, shootbolt locking, cills, couplers. Six lines, 27 units, 72.578 m2, Total Nett Ex VAT\nGBP 34,902.35. It is for PRIDE DEVELOPMENTS, on Severn Trent. Same supplier, same client, six weeks\nold, the same product family as Redditch, and the unit sizes bracket ours from 1.44 m2 up to 6.75 m2.\n The rates fit a clean curve - rate = 721.47 x area to the power -0.4093, R2 of 0.9934 - and I\nre-priced all 43 Redditch items on it.\n That gives GBP 86,354.89 against my benchmark GBP 89,910.77. So my number is about 4% high overall.\n But the interesting part is underneath the total:\n band St Mary's says Severn Trent says\nunder 1.5m2 -35.5% -38.9%\n1.5 - 3 m2 -1.2% -20.4%\n3 - 6 m2 +37.5% +18.1%\nover 6 m2 +35.2% +34.1%\n Positive means my engine sits ABOVE the real price. Two completely independent jobs, different\nsuppliers, different dates - and they agree within a few points on the small band and the large\nband. My register UNDER-prices small windows by roughly a third and OVER-prices big ones by roughly\na third, and those errors partly cancel on a broad job, which is why the total only looks 4% out.\n Redditch is 62% big-unit work by value, so the cancelling is weaker here than usual, and my\nbenchmark is more likely high than low. That is the direction that matters when you are trying to\nundercut somebody.\n So: three estimates of the same job. GBP 89,910.77 on my benchmark, GBP 86,354.89 on the Severn\nTrent curve, GBP 79,047.50 if the St Mary's band corrections are right. The whole spread is the\n3 to 6 m2 band.\n Two honest caveats on the Severn Trent curve, and both push it HIGH: that job is 3005 Wine Red\nmetallic, which carries a colour premium, and its outer pane is 6.8 laminated rather than the 4mm\ntoughened Redditch needs. So GBP 86,355 is probably still a little conservative.\n 4. CAN WE UNDERCUT JOEDAN? NOT ON WHAT I HAVE.\n The number to beat is not GBP 90,687.17. That figure is GROSS of 2.5% main contractor's discount,\nso a main contractor takes it at GBP 88,419.99. That is the target.\n And it includes their strip-out. Ours does not.\n our basis our sell headroom per opening\nmy benchmark 89,910.77 -1,490.78 -34.67\nSevern Trent curve 86,354.89 2,065.10 48.03\nSt Mary's band correction 79,047.50 9,372.50 217.97\n Headroom is what is left to pay for stripping 43 openings out of an occupied public library, same\nday replacement, nothing left out over a shift.\n At my benchmark there is nothing left - we are already over before we start. On the better evidence\nthere is GBP 48 an opening. I do not believe GBP 48 an opening strips out and disposes of a\ncommercial aluminium window, but I cannot prove that either, which brings me back to the same wall.\n 5. THE STRIP-OUT RATE STILL DOES NOT EXIST, AND I HAVE NOW PROVED IT PROPERLY\n\n I searched every workbook in the tender archive - 362 of them - for a priced window removal line.\n I got some promising hits and they were all false. Lines like &quot;Carefully remove existing PVCu doors\nand windows&quot; on Spencer Scroft against the number 30.06, and &quot;remove existing PVCu framed windows&quot;\non Hollyfield against 24.11. Those are ITEM REFERENCE NUMBERS, not rat"
