   "body": "Take-off done. 43 items, 41 references, 136.54 m2, and it is attached as a five-sheet workbook -\nschedule, priced schedule with the source of every rate, inclusions and exclusions, and ten RFIs.\n BENCHMARK NET, EX VAT: GBP 89,910.82\n That figure EXCLUDES strip-out, and that is the first thing you need to know.\n WHERE THE TAKE-OFF COMES FROM\n Not from Joedan. The tender has its own blank pricing schedule at page 77 of the pack, and it is the\nsame 43 rows as Appendix 2 - same references, same sizes, same configurations, rates deleted. So the\nquantities are the client's, not the competitor's, and I have cross-checked every row against the two\nelevational drawings. All 41 references appear on the elevations. Nothing missing, nothing duplicated.\n I priced ours first and only then looked at Joedan's number.\n STRIP-OUT: THE CLIENT HAS ASKED FOR IT AS A SEPARATE LINE AND WE CANNOT FILL IT IN\n Page 70 of the pack ends with two blanks:\n Cost for stripping out windows GBP ...........\nCost for new windows and doors GBP ...........\n We can fill the second one. We cannot fill the first, because there is no strip-out rate anywhere in\nFenster's records. Our installation figure is fit-only - that is proven, not assumed: GBP 39,680 on\nPrincess Beatrice is exactly the per-unit codes over 217 units, and Brocks Hill, a new build with no\nwindow to remove, recomputes from the same codes at the same rates.\n This is the fifth job in a fortnight to hit it - Gordon Court, St Mary's, Princess Beatrice, John North\nHall, now this. I have added it to REQ-24 rather than raise a new request, because it is the same\nquestion you already have open. But this one is different in kind from the other four. On those,\nstrip-out was an inclusion we had implied and would have absorbed. Here it is a numbered blank on the\nclient's own tender form, and a return that leaves it empty is incomplete on its face.\n 43 openings stripped out of an occupied public library, same-day replacement, no reveal left vacant\nover a shift. That is what needs a number.\n HOW IT COMPARES TO JOEDAN, AND WHY THE CLOSENESS IS MISLEADING\n Joedan: GBP 90,687.17 gross of 2.5% main contractor's discount, so GBP 88,419.99 net to a main\ncontractor.\n Ours: GBP 89,910.82. Minus 0.9% on their gross, plus 1.7% on their net.\n Do not take any comfort from that. It is two errors pointing in opposite directions.\n Joedan's price INCLUDES strip-out - their clause 12, removal of the existing elements, disposal by the\nmain contractor. Ours excludes it. So on identical scope we are ALREADY ABOVE Joedan, before a penny of\nstrip-out goes in.\n Pulling the other way: 62% of this job's value sits in the 3 to 6 m2 size band, and that is the band\nwhere the St Mary's calibration measured our register running 37.5% high. Apply those band corrections\nto the supply and the same take-off gives GBP 79,047.55.\n So the honest range is roughly GBP 79k to GBP 90k on frames and fit, plus strip-out, and the benchmark\nis more likely high than accurate. It is not a price to give anybody. Nothing has gone to Pride.\n THE SPECIFICATION CANNOT BE BUILT AT TWO POSITIONS\n This is the one I would want you to see even if you read nothing else.\n References 32 and 34 are each drawn on Elevation C as ONE assembly - a window frame joined directly to\na door frame. The specification puts the windows in EL75mm Squareline, thermally broken, 75mm deep, and\nthe doors in AC100 Commercial, expressly non-thermal, 100mm deep.\n You cannot couple a 75mm frame to a 100mm frame. That is your own ruling from SM5 Wexham on 24 July,\nand this is the same fault - except that on SM5 it was our quotation that did it, and here it is written\ninto the client's specification before anyone has priced anything.\n Your rule says the window takes the door's system. Here that would put both windows into a non-thermal\nframe, which fails the 1.4 W/m2K the same specification demands two clauses later. The rule and the\nspec cannot both be satisfied as written.\n I have priced both runs in a single thermally broken system throughout, which is the only reading that\nsatisfies both, and RFI-03 puts the three possible resolutions back to Gleeds. GBP 7,552.56 rides on it.\n FOUR MORE THINGS FROM THE DRAWINGS THAT ARE NOT IN THE SCHEDULE\n I rendered the elevations rather than trusting the text layer, and four items do not match their rows.\n References 16, 17 and 18 are RAKED. They are parallelograms following the stair soffit - sloping head\nand sloping cill - and the schedule gives them as 2250 x 2304 rectangles, all three identical, which is\na bounding box and not a frame. Joedan priced all three at exactly GBP 2,619.48 each. GBP 8,509 of our\nnumber sits on them.\n Reference 38 is drawn as a five-by-three grid, fifteen panes. The schedule says six fixed lights. Four\nmullions and two transoms is not the same buy as six lights.\n References 29, 30 and 31 have every configuration column blank - on GLEEDS' schedule as well as\nJoedan's, so it is the tender that is silent. Joedan priced them anyway at GBP 640.23 each. I have\npriced them as fixed lights and said so.\n Reference 39 is a door that appears nowhere in the ironmongery clause, which lists 32, 34, 37 and 41.\nNo ironmongery is specified for it anywhere in the pack. Joedan charged GBP 2,621.58, which is GBP 327\nmore than reference 37 at the identical size, so their price carries something ours does not. I have\npriced it as a plain doorset with no panic set.\n ASBESTOS - GOOD NEWS ON THE WINDOWS\n All four window-specific samples came back clean: mastic to the metal framed windows, putty sealant,\nmastic to the window frames, mastic to the window joins. Seventy-five linear metres, no asbestos\ndetected. The stuff we cut through is fine.\n But sample FM000125 is asbestos bitumen paint to metal cladding - chrysotile, 100 m2, recorded as\n&quot;present to left hand elevation&quot;, accessibility &quot;easily disturbed&quot;, with the recommendation &quot;REMOVE if\naffected by scheduled work&quot;. If &quot;left h"
